# Metal Doped Core-Shell Metal-Organic Frameworks@Covalent Organic Frameworks (MOFs@COFs) Hybrids as a Novel Photocatalytic Platform

## abstract
Abstract: Metal doped core–shell Metal‐Organic Frameworks@Covalent Organic Frameworks (MOFs@COFs) are presented as a novel platform for photocatalysis. A palladium (Pd) doped MOFs@COFs in the form of Pd/TiATA@LZU1 shows excellent photocatalytic performance for tandem dehydrogenation and hydrogenation reactions in a continuous‐flow microreactor and a batch system, indicating the great potential of the metal doped MOFs@COFs as a multifunctional platform for photocatalysis. Explanations for the performance enhancement are elucidated. An integrated dual‐chamber microreactor coupled with the metal doped MOFs@COFs is introduced to demonstrate a concept of an intensified green photochemical process, which can be broadly extended to challenging liquid–gas tandem and cascade reactions.

Metal doped core–shell Metal-Organic Frameworks@Covalent Organic Frameworks (MOFs@COFs) are presented as a novel platform for photocatalysis. A palladium (Pd) doped MOFs@COFs in the form of Pd/ TiATA@LZU1 shows excellent photocatalytic performance for tandem dehydrogenation and hydrogenation reactions in a continuous-flow microreactor and a batch system, indicating the great potential of the metal doped MOFs@COFs as a multifunctional platform for photocatalysis. Explanations for the performance enhancement are elucidated. An integrated dual-chamber microreactor coupled with the metal doped MOFs@COFs is introduced to demonstrate a concept of an intensified green photochemical process, which can be broadly extended to challenging liquid–gas tandem and cascade reactions.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of porous materials constructed from metal or metal clusters interconnected by organic ligands. Complex architectures are usually found in MOFs, which enable easy control of their properties for desired applications.[1–3] Improved structural and functional complexity of MOFs can be realized by postsynthetic modification of either the ligands or the metal centers to afford multivariate MOFs. These multivariate MOFs usually show properties superior or unprecedented to their parent MOFs owing to the presence of synergistic effect or increased catalytic active sites.[4–8] Additional levels of MOF complexity can be achieved by hybridization of MOFs with another functional material such as metal nanoparticles, enzymes, metalloporphyrin, and metal–organic complex.[9–15] These hybrid materials can possess contradictory properties that their counterparts can never achieve. The conjugation of one type of MOFs with another type of MOFs, in particular, to form core–shell MOFs@MOFs hybrids has received intensive interest because of the more possibilities for the structural and functional modulation rendered by the combined MOF complexities.[16–19]
Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) have also emerged subsequently as a new class of crystalline porous materials. They are constructed with light elements by strong covalent bonds based on the reticular chemistry and have shown great potential in diverse applications.[20–24] 2D imine-based COFs stand out among COFs because of their high stability.[25] The successful demonstration of the core–shell MOFs $@$ MOFs inspired us to look into the synthesis of core–shell MOFs $@$ COFs, which still remains largely unexplored. Although a MOFs/COFs composite membrane has been used for a gas separation,[26] and a synthesized hybrid core–shell MOF $@$ COF has very recently been shown to be effective in photocatalytic degradation of a dye,[27] we were motivated by several advantages that the hybrid can offer, in particular, a metal doped hybrid MOFs $@$ COFs. First of all, the presence of the homogeneously distributed heteroatoms (N) in 2D imine-based COFs allows them to serve as ideal platforms for anchoring metal species.[28] Owing to the nearly eclipsed and ordered $\pi$ -columnar structures as well as the large pore size, these metal sites are much more easily accessible by the substrates than those in metal/MOFs. Therefore, a larger amount of exposed active metal sites can be provided by metal/MOFs $@$ COFs than by metal/MOFs@MOFs. Second, a high photoconductivity and/or charge mobility usually exists in COFs due to the well stacked $\pi$ arrays in contrast to the poor electronic conductivity of MOFs, and therefore, the COF shell can act as an electron mediator for promoting the charge transfer from MOFs to metal sites.[29–31] Third, a strong chemical interaction between the MOF core and the COF shell can easily be realized by imparting specific functionalities because of the tunable nature of both MOFs and COFs,[32–34] which could protect the COF layers from slipping off, a common problem of 2D materials. Therefore, it is anticipated that construction of core–shell MOFs@COFs could overcome their inherent weak points and in the meantime create synergistic effect to afford multifunctional properties for specific applications.
Photocatalysis has been regarded as a promising green approach to solving energy and environmental issues.[35–37]
MOFs have recent years emerged as a new class of promising photocatatic material owing to their unique properties.[14,38–42] Among the various MOFs, Ti-based MOFs appear to be very attractive candidates in photocatalysis due to their good chemical stability, redox activity, and photocatalytic properties.[43,44] $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) (TiATA) represents one of the most widely examined photoactive Ti-MOFs, which has shown great potential in various photocatalytic applications such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, hydrogen evolution as well as amine and alcohol oxidation.[45–51] Herein, we reported the first demonstration of metal doped core–shell MOFs $@$ COFs as a novel multifunctional platform for photocatalysis. For the purpose, a palladium (Pd) doped core–shell TiATA $@$ LZU1 (Pd/TiATA@LZU1) was fabricated, in which the LZU1 refers to the 2D iminie-based COF-LZU1. Note that the use of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ containing MOFs allows direct growing COF shell without extra functionalization step. To demonstrate the usefulness of the Pd doped MOF ${\tt s@C O F}$ hybrid, we conducted the photocatalytic hydrogenation of olefins and the photocatalytic dehydrogenation of ammonia borane $(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ , AB), respectively, under visible light. We believe that this conceptual metal doped core–shell MOFs $@$ COFs could open a promising avenue for developing efficient photocatalysts through engineering of combinatorial design of molecule/cluster activators and diverse porous frameworks.
Moreover, photocatalytic tandem dehydrogenation of AB and hydrogenation of olefins was realized in a newly designed dualchamber microreactor by elaborately immobilizing Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 on the inner wall. In situ generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas in the bottom chamber instantly passed through gas-permeable membrane to the upper chamber for consumption by hydrogenation. This photochemical process in the dual-chamber microreactor prevents the product contamination and avoids the mismatching problem of the two different reaction conditions, which often occur in bulk reactors. The dual-chamber concept could be further extended to other challenging liquid–gas tandem and cascade reactions, especially for those reactions involving toxic or dangerous gases.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The core–shell MOFs $@$ COFs hybrid was prepared by facile room temperature synthesis (Scheme 1). Both $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) MOF, denoted as TiATA (pore size: ${\approx}0.6$ and $1.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), and COF-LZU1, denoted as LZU1 (pore size: ${\approx}1.8~\mathrm{nm}$ ), show relatively crystalline XRD patterns, consistent with the reported results (Figure S1, Supporting Information).[28,45,51] The core– shell TiATA $@$ LZU1 hybrid exhibits a pattern similar to TiATA owing to the weak intensity of LZU1. The FT-IR spectrum of the hybridized product matches well with either TiATA or LZU1 (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The presence of the typical stretching band for the $-C=\mathrm{N}$ at $\approx1619~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicates the formation of imine-linked bonds. The $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ CP/MAS NMR spectrum of the hybridized product is almost identical to that of TiATA and LZU1, further confirming the formation of LZU1 shell and the structural preservation of TiATA core during the hybridization process (Figure S3, Supporting Information).
The morphology of the MOFs $@$ COFs hybrid was investigated by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Figure 1a,b and Figure S4a,b (Supporting Information) show that the pristine TiATA nanoparticles are in octahedral shapes with smooth surface and that the LZU1 has a layered-sheet morphology. The SEM images of the MOFs $@$ COFs hybrid reveal that the octahedral shape of TiATA well retained during the hybridization process because the amount of amino groups on the surface of TiATA is deficient to confine the aldehyde of COF precursor onto MOFs. To gain more insight into the role of the amino groups, the electronic state of N in TiATA $@$ LZU1 was studied by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses. The XPS spectrum of TiATA $@$ LZU1 in N 1s region exhibits five peaks at 396.6, 398.4, 399.5, 401.7, and $402.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information). The peaks at 399.5 and $402.9\mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to the amino groups in TiATA core, while the other three peaks correspond to the N in LZU1 shell. Compared with the pristine TiATA, TiATA $@$ LZU1 shows a shift of the binding energy from $399.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ over TiATA to $399.5\mathrm{eV},$ indicating the interaction between the amino groups in TiATA core and the aldehyde groups in LZU1 shell. For a control experiment, TiBDC, an isoreticular analog of TiATA, was used to form a core– shell TiBDC@LZU1 that does not involve any interfacial interaction, and a mixture of the hybrid with self-agglomerated LZU1 nanoparticles was produced (Figure S10a–c, Supporting Information). After one month of exposure to air, most of the LZU1 shell on TiBDC $@$ LZU1 nanoparticles peeled off (Figure S10d, Supporting Information), while the core–shell structure of TiATA $@$ LZU1 could be well preserved even after six months. These contrasting results prove the critical role of the amino groups of TiATA in stabilizing the TiATA $@$ LZU1 core–shell (Figure 1c; Figure S4c, Supporting Information). In contrast to the smooth surface of pristine TiATA, the surface of TiATA in the MOFs $@$ COFs hybrid is well covered by the vertically grown COF nanosheets (Figure 1c,d; Figure S4c,d, Supporting Information). The presence of the (001) facet of LZU1 in the XRD patterns of the MOFs $@$ COFs hybrid suggests that these 2D nanosheets have been extended to 3D structure (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The TiATA $@$ LZU1 core–shell particles in size between 0.5 and $1.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ formed a shell with ${\approx}20~\mathrm{nm}$ thick, as confirmed by EDX line data and elemental mapping images (Figure S5, Supporting Information; Figure 1e). The weight ratio of TiATA:LZU1 in TiATA $@$ LZU1 is determined to be ${\approx}3.3$ based on the TG analyses (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Similar with TiATA, TiATA $@$ LZU1 also shows two main absorption peaks at around 280 and $375\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S7, Supporting Information). However, as compared with TiATA, the peak at around $370\mathrm{nm}$ is a little broader in TiATA $@$ LZU1, which is due to the absorption of LZU1.
The ratio of MOF core to COF shell has a great influence on the formation of highly qualified core–shell TiATA $@$ LZU1. Figure S8 of the Supporting Information shows that only part of the TiATA nanoparticles are covered by the LZU1 sheets when the concentration of the LZU1 precursor is low. When the concentration is high, on the other hand, both TiATA $@$ LZU1 and agglomerated LZU1 nanoparticles form presumably structure. It is worth noting that unlike the previous report, this interfacial seeding control strategy by imparting $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ containing MOFs is an effective approach for synthesizing various MOFs/COFs nanohybrids without extra functionalization step.
The periodic arrangement of the imine bonds and the eclipsed $\pi$ -columnar structures make 2D imine-based COFs an appealing platform for incorporating active metal sites. The metal doped MOFs $@$ COFs ternary nanohybrids could be a promising new platform for multifunctional photocatalysis with the photocatalytic property of MOFs and the good electronic conductivity of COFs. For the metal, Pd was chosen owing to its excellent catalytic behavior in various kinds of reactions. The $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ was incorporated into TiATA $@$ LZU1 via a simple post-treatment of TiATA $@$ LZU1 with palladium acetate. The XPS spectrum of the $\mathrm{Pd^{2+}/T i A T A@L Z U1}$ in Pd 3d region shows the binding energy of Pd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ at $337.6\ \mathrm{eV},$ which is comparable with that of $\mathrm{Pd^{2+}}/\mathrm{LZU1}$ in the previous report, indicating the strong coordination of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ with the imine groups of LZU1 shell (Figure S11, Supporting Information).[28] After treating with $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ , the $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ was reduced to $\mathrm{Pd}^{0}$ as confirmed by the XPS spectrum, which shows the binding energy of Pd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ at $335.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Owing to the surface oxidation of Pd nanoparticles, a small amount of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ is also observed in the Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1. A comparison of the XRD patterns and FT-IR spectra between
Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 and TiATA $@$ LZU1 reveals that the structure of TiATA $@$ LZU1 is well preserved during the Pd doping process (Figures S12 and S13, Supporting Information). The core– shell structure of TiATA $@$ LZU1 also remains unchanged as confirmed by the TEM images (Figure 2a). The enlarged TEM image shows that Pd nanoparticles with an average diameter of $2.2\mathrm{nm}$ are well dispersed on the COF shell (Figure 2b). The amount of Pd in Pd/TiATA@LZU1 is determined to be $3.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ by ICP-OES analysis. Pd/TiATA@LZU1 shows similar light absorption ability as compared with TiATA $@$ LZU1, indicating the negligible effect of Pd on the light absorption property (Figure S7, Supporting Information).
With the time stability established, we tested the Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 for its efficiency in the photocatalytic hydrogenation of olefins, an important transformation in industrial applications, under visible light in a bulk reactor. As shown in Table 1, entry 1, full conversion of styrene to ethylbenzene with a high selectivity of $599\%$ was achieved in only $15~\mathrm{min}$ under visible light irradiation. The turnover frequency (TOF) is calculated to be $589~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which is higher than that of most Pd/MOFs in the previous reports (Table S1, Supporting Information). There was no reaction at all over TiATA $@$ LZU1 or in the absence of Pd/TiATA $@{\mathrm{LZU1}}$ , indicating that Pd metal is the active site for the reaction. A much lower styrene conversion of $38\%$ was observed when the reaction was carried out in dark, which is evident that visible light can greatly promote the hydrogenation reaction over Pd/TiATA $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{LZU1}$ (Table 1, entry 2). In addition, no reaction under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ excluded the possibility that visible light irradiation induced the hydrogen evolution from ethanol for the hydrogenation reaction. The reaction in a nonproton solvent like N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) proceeded to styrene conversion of $43.5\%$ in $15~\mathrm{min}$ , further confirming that the proton source is $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Table 1, entry 3). The lower catalytic activity in NMP is attributed to absence of the enhanced proton transfer in a proton solvent ethanol. It has been well documented that the electronic state of the metal catalysts considerably affects the hydrogenation reaction, in which higher electron density around metal sites could promote the reaction by facilitating the activation of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and olefins.[52,53] Since excited TiATA by visible light released and transferred electrons to Pd,[46] the light enhanced performance of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 should be explained by the increased electron density around Pd. During the reaction, Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 did not leach with no detection of Pd in the filtrate as measured by ICP-OES and no aggregation among the doped Pd nanoparticles as seen in TEM images, probably due to its strong coordination with the imine groups in COF shell (Figure S14, Supporting Information). The high durability and reusability of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 were further confirmed by the XRD and FT-IR analyses as well as the cycling test (Figures S15–S17, Supporting Information). As a comparison, the reaction over Pd/LZU1 and Pd/TiATA gave only low conversions of $42\%$ and $83\%$ , respectively (Table 1, entry 4–5). The superior activity of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 in photocatalytic performance to both Pd/LZU1 and Pd/TiATA indicates the presumable presence of synergistic effects among the TiATA core, the LZU1 shell, and the doped Pd metal.
To investigate the synergistic effects of the metal doped MOF core and COF shell system in Pd/TiATA@LZU1, photoluminescence (PL) was used to study the electron transfer process.
As shown in Figure 3, TiATA exhibits a broad luminescence band centered at $434~\mathrm{nm}$ with the excitation at $380~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is attributed to the excitation of ATA ligand in the TiATA. The presence of LZU1 shell dramatically quenches the emission, indicating that the LZU1 shell promotes the charge separation in the TiATA core. Compared with the PL of TiATA, a red shift of the luminescence by $33~\mathrm{nm}$ is observed over TiATA $@$ LZU1. This phenomenon can be explained by the strong interaction of the amino groups in the TiATA core with the electron withdrawing aldehyde groups in the LZU1 shell, and the increased degree of conjugation due to the $\pi$ -conjugated system of LZU1.[54,55] The luminescence can be further quenched by Pd incorporation into TiATA $@$ LZU1, as evidenced by the weaker luminescence of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1. Moreover, Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 also shows a luminescence band much lower than Pd/ TiATA, revealing that the LZU1 shell functions as an electron mediator to facilitate the electron transfer from the TiATA core to Pd sites. Based on the above results, a possible mechanism for the hydrogenation of olefins over Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 is proposed. Upon visible light irradiation, the TiATA core is excited to generate electrons. The presence of the LZU1 shell as an electronic mediator promotes the electron transfer from the excited TiATA to Pd for the hydrogenation reactions. Since the $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ would be reduced by the light excited TiATA during the reaction, the Pd nanoparticles should be the main active sites. In addition to this “donor-mediator-acceptor” system, the porous structure of both TiATA and LZU1 promotes mass transfer for facile accessibility of substrates into the metal sites on the LZU1 shell, leading to excellent photocatalytic activity of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1. These results suggest that this conceptual metal doped core–shell MOFs $@$ COFs would be a novel platform for efficient photocatalysts.
The hydrogenation reaction over Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 can be expanded to various kinds of olefins. As shown in Table S2 of the Supporting Information, both the allylbenzene and the trans- $\cdot\beta\cdot$ -methylstyrene can be fully converted to propylbenzene with a high selectivity of $599\%$ in $15~\mathrm{min}$ (Table S2, entry 2–3,
Supporting Information). In addition to the aromatic olefins, Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 is also active for the hydrogenation of aliphatic olefins, but longer time is needed as compared with the aromatic ones. Cyclohexene is converted to cyclohexane in $90\ \mathrm{min}$ with a high conversion of $91\%$ and selectivity of $599\%$ (Table S2, entry 4, Supporting Information). The selective hydrogenation of olefins with the presence of other functional groups is much more interesting owing to its importance for synthesizing additives and flavors. As shown in Table S2, entry 5 (Supporting Information), a complete selective conversion of 5-hexen-2-one to 2-hexanone can also be realized over Pd/TiATA $_{\textcircled{a}\mathrm{LZU1}}$ in 45 min in spite of the coexistence of both olefinic bond and carbonyl group, suggesting the great promise of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 for the selective hydrogenation of olefins bearing different kinds of unsaturated groups.
For hydrogenation in general, efficient and safe storage and handling of gaseous $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are key issues. One of the best solutions is to use solid or liquid hydrogen donor to replace the gaseous $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Due to the high hydrogen content $(19.6~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , high stability, and nontoxicity properties, ammonia borane has been considered as a promising chemical hydrogen-storage material, from which hydrogen can be released through dehydrogenation.[56] Therefore, it is desirable to achieve efficient hydrogenation reactions by taking advantage of in situ generated hydrogen from AB.[57,58] Prior to the tandem hydrogenation reaction with AB as the hydrogen source, the feasibility of Pd/TiATA $@\mathrm{LZU1}$ for photocatalytic dehydrogenation of AB for hydrogen was tested. As shown in Figure 4a, complete evolution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with an $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{AB}$ molar ratio of 3.0 can be achieved in $55~\mathrm{min}$ over Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 in dark. Visible light significantly promotes the reaction to be completed in only $8~\mathrm{min}$ , presumably due to the formation of electron rich Pd sites and the production of superoxide anions and hydroxide anions, which can facilitate the activation of AB.[59,60] No reaction was observed over TiATA $@$ LZU1 under visible light, indicating that Pd is the active site (Figure 4a). It was found that the hydrogen evolution rate increased after reacting for around $4~\mathrm{min}$ , which is due to the reduction of the $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ during the reaction.[58] After first run reaction, the performance of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 for the dehydrogenation of AB becomes almost steady and the reaction time can be shortened to 6 min, corresponding to a high TOF of $147~\mathrm{mol}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2})\ \mathrm{min^{-1}\ m o l^{-1}}$ (Pd) (Figure 4b). As far as we know, this value is one of the highest among the previously reported mono-Pd based catalysts and is also higher than most of the Pd-based multimetal catalysts (Table S3, Supporting Information). The striking activity of Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 could be attributed to the synergistic effect among the MOF core and the Pd nanoparticles well dispersed into COF shell. The Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 is highly stable during the reaction as confirmed by XRD and FT-IR analyses as well as the filtrate experiment and cycling test (Figure 4; Figures S18 and S19, Supporting Information).
The excellent photocatalytic performance of the Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 for both hydrogenation of olefins and the dehydrogenation of AB prompted us to investigate the tandem dehydrogenation and hydrogenation reactions. The general practice for a tandem or cascade hydrogenation reaction is to mix directly the hydrogen donors and the substrates in one bulk reactor, called one-pot reaction. This practice may lead to the contamination of products and thus require tedious separation steps for purification.[57,58,61] Moreover, since the conditions required for the two reactions are usually different, their implementation in one-pot reactor may cause side reactions or suppression of both sides. An effective way to avoid these problems is to perform the two reactions in separated spaces with gas selective permeation.
## 3. Conclusions
We have presented a unique photocatalytic platform of metal doped core–shell MOFs $@$ COFs. Synthesis of the platform has been shown to be simple and direct, not involving any extra functionalization of MOFs for the synthesis. The Pd doped TiATA $@$ LZU1 or Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 tested for photocatalysis has revealed that a synergy exists among the three components with the metal elucidated to be the active center, the MOF core as an electron donor, and the COF shell as a mediator for electron transfer. The emergence of this “donor–mediator–acceptor” system of metal doped MOFs $@$ COFs for enhanced photocatalysis would be significant for the design of different efficient MOFs $@$ COFs-based photocatalysts due to the tunable and flexible chemistry of both MOFs and COFs. The Pd/TiATA $@$ LZU1 system has been shown to be quite effective for both hydrogenation and dehydrogenation. An integrated dual-chamber microreactor coupled with the catalyst system for tandem dehydrogenation and hydrogenation reactions has been introduced to demonstrate a concept of intensified green photochemical process, which can be broadly extended to challenging liquid–gas tandem and cascade reactions.
